1
00:00:00,389 --> 00:00:02,989
The report says zero orders slip
through.

2
00:00:03,064 --> 00:00:05,814
Let’s translate that into a call we can
test.

3
00:00:05,864 --> 00:00:09,167
When the validator receives zero, it
currently returns true.

4
00:00:09,779 --> 00:00:11,629
The issue requires false.

5
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:13,954
The inputs one and five should still
pass,

6
00:00:14,004 --> 00:00:16,229
and negative one should still fail.

7
00:00:16,304 --> 00:00:19,654
Keep that expected behavior in view
while we investigate what the code

8
00:00:19,704 --> 00:00:20,565
actually does.

9
00:00:22,106 --> 00:00:26,231
Run the existing tests from the training
checkout in the approved environment.

10
00:00:26,281 --> 00:00:27,781
The suite goes green.

11
00:00:27,866 --> 00:00:29,229
Now read the test names.

12
00:00:29,853 --> 00:00:33,353
There’s one test each for one, five, and
negative one.

13
00:00:33,403 --> 00:00:35,053
There’s no zero case.

14
00:00:35,103 --> 00:00:37,716
That's why a green result can coexist
with the bug.

15
00:00:38,365 --> 00:00:42,465
Record the checkout and the exact test
command so we can repeat the check

16
00:00:42,515 --> 00:00:43,324
consistently.

17
00:00:44,810 --> 00:00:48,875
Open the issue, the validator, and the
tests in VS Code.

18
00:00:48,950 --> 00:00:52,962
In Copilot Plan, ask which expression
could explain this behavior.

19
00:00:53,561 --> 00:00:56,961
The proposed cause is the greater-than-
or-equal comparison.

20
00:00:57,036 --> 00:00:59,286
Read the line and substitute zero.

21
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:01,940
Zero equals zero, so the expression is
true.

22
00:01:02,599 --> 00:01:04,324
Now we have a prediction.

23
00:01:04,399 --> 00:01:08,326
An assertion expecting false for zero
should fail on the original revision.

24
00:01:09,835 --> 00:01:14,135
Ask Agent to add just the zero
regression to the existing test class.

25
00:01:14,210 --> 00:01:16,110
Inspect the proposed patch.

26
00:01:16,185 --> 00:01:19,380
It should call the public function with
zero and expect false.

27
00:01:19,972 --> 00:01:23,897
The validator itself should still have
its original comparison.

28
00:01:23,972 --> 00:01:27,747
This separation is useful because the
next run will tell us whether the new

29
00:01:27,797 --> 00:01:31,761
test can detect the reported behavior
before any fix is applied.

30
00:01:33,272 --> 00:01:35,897
Run the same suite with the new test
present.

31
00:01:35,972 --> 00:01:39,197
Look for the zero test name and the
assertion message.

32
00:01:39,272 --> 00:01:41,558
True was returned where false was
expected.

33
00:01:42,151 --> 00:01:44,626
That failure supports the hypothesis.

34
00:01:44,701 --> 00:01:47,126
If the suite stops because of a missing
import,

35
00:01:47,176 --> 00:01:49,826
it hasn't shown that the boundary is
wrong.

36
00:01:49,901 --> 00:01:54,281
Fix the setup first and rerun until you
have the failure you intended to test.

37
00:01:55,768 --> 00:01:59,468
Change greater than or equal to zero to
greater than zero.

38
00:01:59,543 --> 00:02:02,693
Keep the assertion and the function
signature as they are.

39
00:02:02,768 --> 00:02:05,743
Rerun the same test command on the
updated checkout.

40
00:02:06,402 --> 00:02:08,602
Now all four cases should pass.

41
00:02:08,652 --> 00:02:11,402
Check the source diff and the test diff
together.

42
00:02:11,477 --> 00:02:14,198
Each one should answer a part of the
issue you started with.

43
00:02:15,668 --> 00:02:19,843
Let’s turn the investigation into
something another engineer can replay.

44
00:02:19,918 --> 00:02:24,587
Record the zero input, the original
comparison, and both test results.

45
00:02:25,246 --> 00:02:27,746
Say which revision produced each output.

46
00:02:27,796 --> 00:02:31,871
Then explain why one still passes and
negative one still fails.

47
00:02:31,946 --> 00:02:34,321
If you can answer from the source and
the tests,

48
00:02:34,371 --> 00:02:37,586
you've verified the change as well as
the proposed diagnosis.
